<Post title="Vendor software on my hardware" part={2} date="2026-08-16">
	<p class="tagline">
		Step one of de-locking the system: keep the vendor's apps, but run them on hardware and
		an OS that I control. Enter a 2015 Samsung Galaxy Tab A 8.0.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>why a decade-old tablet</h2>
	<p>
		The OEM wall unit is, at its heart, a low-end 8" Android tablet with a custom
		power/data connector. Its jobs are modest: render the MyPlace UI, run the vendor's
		background service, and talk to the control box. A <strong>Samsung Galaxy Tab A 8.0
		(SM-T350, <code>gt58wifi</code>)</strong> from 2015 — which I already had on hand —
		is overqualified for all three. It also has something the OEM tablet doesn't: an
		unlockable bootloader and a working custom-ROM scene.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the stack</h2>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>layer</th><th>choice</th><th>why</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>OS</td>
				<td>LineageOS 18.1 (Android 11), unofficial <code>gt58wifi</code> build</td>
				<td>Modern-ish Android on 2015 hardware; no Google services required</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Root</td>
				<td>Magisk 30.7, Zygisk on</td>
				<td>Systemless root + module framework for the appliance scripts</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Remote access</td>
				<td>SimpleSSHD on port 2222, key-only</td>
				<td>The tablet is headless infrastructure; SSH is the admin interface</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Vendor apps</td>
				<td><code>myair5</code> (MyPlace UI) + <code>aaservice2</code> (background service)</td>
				<td>The actual control surface, kept intact</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Network</td>
				<td>Dedicated IoT VLAN, static DHCP reservation</td>
				<td>Vendor apps get no route to anything they don't need</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the one check that matters</h2>
	<p>
		The vendor apps refuse to run on hardware they don't recognise — the infamous
		"Not on AA Hardware" toast. The check is a string comparison against
		<code>Build.MODEL</code>, and the accepted model for the 8" MyAir5 tablet is
		<code>PIC8GS10</code>. A comment on the Hacker News thread for the
		<a href="https://blog.hopefullyuseful.com/blog/advantage-air-ezone-tablet-diy-repair/"
			>upstream repair story</a
		>
		had already pointed out that on a rooted tablet you can just change the model prop and
		everything works — and that's exactly the approach:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>MagiskHide Props Config</strong> module, configured with a late-stage custom
			prop: <code>ro.product.model=PIC8GS10</code>
		</li>
		<li>
			A belt-and-braces Magisk boot script that <code>resetprop</code>s the same value
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		No APK modification needed for this step — the apps verify the device's identity, and
		root lets the device lie about it fluently:
	</p>
	<pre><code>$ ssh tablet
$ su -c 'getprop ro.product.model'
PIC8GS10</code></pre>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>making it an appliance</h2>
	<p>
		A wall-mounted control panel has a different reliability bar to a personal tablet. It's
		powered 24/7, must survive reboots unattended, and should need zero maintenance. The
		Magisk <code>service.d</code> boot scripts handle this:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Battery hold at 40–45%</strong> — the tablet runs wall-powered forever, so a
			boot loop script toggles <code>batt_slate_mode</code> to keep the Li-ion in its happy
			band instead of cooking at 100%
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Watchdog</strong> — every 5 s, relaunch the MyPlace UI if it's missing, and
			start the vendor service if <code>dumpsys</code> shows it absent (the service has no
			boot receiver, so process presence alone isn't enough)
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Doze whitelist + background appops</strong> — the battery-hold behaviour
			makes Android think the tablet is "on battery", which invites Doze; both vendor apps
			get whitelisted
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>zygisk-detach</strong> — detaches both vendor packages from the Play Store
			so an unattended "update" can't replace the repackaged apps (see part 3)
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Telemetry</strong> — battery and system metrics pushed to InfluxDB every
			60 s, graphed in Grafana. The control panel is monitored like any other host in the
			house.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>RAM-only logging</strong> — logd buffers sized and kept in RAM; nothing
			wears out the SD card, nothing persists across reboot
		</li>
	</ul>

	<div class="callout">
		<span class="callout-label">reproducibility</span>
		The full rebuild runbook — every script, every setting, every APK version — lives in my
		private infrastructure docs, so a brand-new identical tablet can be brought to the same
		state from scratch. The details above are the transferable parts; the omitted bits are
		just my network specifics.
	</div>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>where this leaves us</h2>
	<p>
		At this point the system is <em>functionally</em> stock — same apps, same UI, same
		features — but every layer underneath the apps is mine. That buys two things: the
		tablet is now replaceable commodity hardware, and root access opens the door to the
		next step. Because the vendor apps still enforce a signature permission on their
		inter-app broadcasts, and my own service implementation can't hold the vendor's signing
		key. That's part 3.
	</p>
</Post>
